\section{Introduction}
Although structural genomics initiatives have revealed a wide array of unexpected protein topologies, knotted proteins were long considered a curiosity with minimal functional impact. However, recent discoveries have shown that protein knots are more prevalent than previously thought and appear to be evolving rapidly. Current evidence indicates that both eukaryotic and prokaryotic organisms harbor diverse types of protein knots, which are distributed evenly among proteins with different functions. Despite this diversity, existing research suggests that these topologically complex structures do not hinder folding kinetics or stability. Nevertheless, questions persist regarding the evolutionary drivers behind knotting and its potential adaptive advantages.
In this study, we investigate protein knots from the perspectives of sequence complexity and contact order. Our analysis reveals that protein complexity is not uniform but is instead localized at the knot, supporting the hypothesis that these proteins fold through a straightforward mechanism.
\section{Background}
\subsection{Topological Classification of Proteins}
The topology of a protein is defined by the spatial arrangement of its polypeptide chain. When the backbone forms a nontrivial knot, the protein is classified as a knotted protein. Typically, only the central portion of the chain is knotted, while the N- and C-termini remain unknotted and extend outward. Topological classification involves analyzing the topology of closed loops generated by connecting the termini. For instance, when a protein adopts a topology of $5_1$, it means the closed loop resembles a trefoil knot.
